Vulnerability 1: Hard-coded Secret in the code
Hard-coded secrets refer to sensitive information—such as passwords, private keys, API tokens, or encryption keys—embedded directly into an application's source code or configuration files, exposing credentials to anyone with repository or binary access.
It was observed that Passwords, Private Keys, and Secret Keys are hardcoded directly within the application code.
Application Source Code / Repository
Hardcoding sensitive information: Hardcoding sensitive information like passwords or encryption keys can expose the information to attackers. Making it difficult to fix: Hardcoding credentials/passwords makes it difficult to rotate or fix the problem. Executing arbitrary code: Hardcoded credentials/passwords can allow attackers to gain unauthorized access to accounts or execute arbitrary code.
• Revoke the secret: Revoke any leaked secrets and remove them from the application source code.
• Use a secret vault: A secret vault should be used to generate and store the new secret. This will ensure the secret’s security and prevent any further unexpected disclosure.
• Store the credentials in a configuration file that is not pushed to the code repository.
• Store the credentials in a database.
• Use your cloud provider’s service for managing secrets.
Ex: SECRET_KEY = os.environ["SECRET_KEY"]
• https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798
• https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/258
Vulnerability 2: SSL/TLS Hostname Verification Disabled
SSL/TLS hostname verification ensures that the domain name in the certificate presented by the remote server matches the hostname the client intended to connect to, preventing traffic interception and identity spoofing.
Application disables SSL/TLS hostname verification, preventing it from verifying whether the server's certificate hostname matches the intended destination.
Application Network Client / HTTPS Connection Configuration
An attacker capable of intercepting network traffic may impersonate the intended server and conduct man-in-the-middle attacks, potentially exposing sensitive information or compromising data integrity.
• Enable strict SSL/TLS hostname verification using the default hostname verifier.
• Ensure that certificate-chain validation remains enabled to establish secure and authenticated HTTPS connections.
• https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/297.html
Vulnerability 3: Improper Certificate Validation
Improper certificate validation occurs when an application fails to validate the trust chain, signature, or validity period of the server's SSL/TLS certificate, allowing connections to untrusted or self-signed certificates.
Application disables SSL/TLS certificate validation by setting verify=False in the HTTP POST request, preventing the client from properly verifying the server's SSL/TLS certificate.
Application HTTP Request Handler / Client Module
An attacker capable of intercepting network traffic may impersonate the intended server and conduct man-in-the-middle attacks, potentially exposing sensitive information or modifying data exchanged between the application and the server.
• Enable SSL/TLS certificate verification by removing verify=False or setting verify=True in the HTTP request.
• Ensure the server presents a valid certificate issued by a trusted Certificate Authority and configure the appropriate CA certificate when necessary.
• https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/295.html
Vulnerability 4: Vulnerable and Outdated Libraries
Using vulnerable and outdated third-party software components occurs when an application depends on libraries or modules that contain publicly disclosed security flaws, allowing known attack vectors to be exploited.
Application is using outdated or vulnerable versions of third-party libraries such as react-router, axios, svgo, jspdf, and qs.
Client-side Application Dependencies / package.json
Exploitation of known vulnerabilities leading to data leakage or unauthorized access. Potential Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) or prototype pollution attacks depending on the library vulnerability. Increased risk due to publicly available exploits targeting outdated versions.
• Upgrade vulnerable libraries to secure, supported versions.
• Avoid using deprecated or vulnerable versions identified in security advisories.
• Implement automated dependency scanning tools in the CI/CD pipeline to catch known vulnerabilities prior to deployment.
• https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html
• https://owasp.org/Top10/A06_2021-Vulnerable_and_Outdated_Components/
Vulnerability 5: Clear Text Logging of Sensitive Information
Clear text logging occurs when confidential data—such as credentials, session tokens, or personal identifiers—is written unmasked into console logs or log files, exposing sensitive details to anyone with log read access.
Sensitive pieces of information are being printed directly to the console in plaintext.
Application Logging Mechanism / Console Output
Information disclosure can be intentional or unintentional and can lead to fraud, identity theft, or reputational damage.
• Avoid logging the full file path or filename of sensitive service account credentials, as it can disclose information about your environment or file system structure.
• Log only the environment name or a generic success message, and restrict logging of sensitive operations to non-production environments to minimize the risk of information disclosure through logs.
• Implement log masking filters for any parameters containing sensitive user credentials.
• https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/353
• https://owasp.org/Top10/A08_2021-Software_and_Data_Integrity_Failures/
Vulnerability 6: Using Clear-text protocols
Clear-text protocols transmit application data without cryptographic encryption, allowing intermediate network devices or attackers on the transmission path to eavesdrop, tamper with, or hijack network sessions.
The application initiates network communication or passes URL arguments using unencrypted clear-text protocols rather than enforcing secure transport channels.
Application Network Communication / URL Configuration
Attackers can intercept and modify traffic, redirect users to malicious endpoints, inject malware, or execute unauthorized code. Even in isolated networks, insider threats and compromised network components can lead to credential theft or data leaks.
• Ensure the application uses the secure HTTPS protocol; modify the logic to check and enforce https:// as the default protocol when it is missing.
• Make application data transit over a secure, authenticated, and encrypted protocol like TLS or SSH.
• Explicitly configure secure endpoints, for example: args: ["https://example.com/"].
• https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200
• https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319
Vulnerability 7: Missing Subresource Integrity (SRI)
Subresource Integrity (SRI) is a browser security mechanism that validates cryptographic hashes of remotely fetched resources (such as scripts from third-party CDNs) to guarantee they have not been maliciously modified or compromised.
Remote third-party scripts and artifacts are included in the application without Subresource Integrity (SRI) attributes, loading unverified external code.
Web Frontend Templates / CDN Script Embeds
On the client side, malicious scripts can impersonate users, steal sensitive data, inject malware, or disrupt application availability. On the server side, compromised software can manipulate business data, escalate privileges, or be used as a pivot for broader network attacks.
• Always use Subresource Integrity (SRI) when loading external resources to verify that the file being loaded has not been altered by malicious actors.
• Prefer self-hosting critical third-party scripts instead of relying on external CDNs to minimize supply chain risks.
• Example implementation:
<script src="https://cdn.example.com/v5.3.6/script.js" integrity="sha384-oqVuAfXRKap7fdgcCY5uykM6+R9GqQ8K/uxy9rx7HNQlGYl1kPzQho1wx4JwY8wC"></script>
• https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/353
• https://owasp.org/Top10/A08_2021-Software_and_Data_Integrity_Failures/
Vulnerability 8: Weak TLS Certificate / Configuration
Weak TLS protocol configuration occurs when an application initializes cryptographic transport without enforcing modern protocol versions or strong cipher suites, exposing connections to protocol downgrade attacks.
Application initializes an SSL/TLS context using the generic "TLS" protocol identifier without explicitly restricting connections to approved TLS versions or specifying secure cipher configurations.
Application SSL/TLS Context Initialization / JVM Runtime Settings
If outdated or insecure TLS protocols or cipher suites are negotiated, communications may be vulnerable to cryptographic attacks, potentially compromising the confidentiality and integrity of transmitted data.
• Explicitly enforce TLS 1.2 or TLS 1.3, disable obsolete protocols (SSLv2, SSLv3, TLS 1.0, TLS 1.1) and weak cipher suites.
• Ensure that the application uses the JVM's secure default trust store to validate server certificates.
• Verify the effective TLS configuration in runtime deployment.
• https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html
Vulnerability 9: Sensitive Information Disclosed
Sensitive information disclosure in source code involves the unintentional inclusion of Personally Identifiable Information (PII) or internal organizational contacts directly inside application files, exposing them across revision history.
A personal/support phone number was found hardcoded directly in the application's source code.
Application Source Code Repository
Attackers may use the exposed phone number for social engineering or impersonation. If the number belongs to an admin or support contact, targeted attacks become easier. The exposure may violate privacy regulations and lead to compliance issues, increasing security, privacy, and reputational risk.
• Remove the phone number from the source code and load it from secure configuration files or backend parameters.
• Use environment variables or a configuration management system to handle sensitive PII.
• Clean the Git commit history to purge previously committed values.
• Implement secure coding guidelines and pre-commit hooks to block hardcoded sensitive information.
• https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure
